;;
;; REPL/RedTeam/[RT-H]_InputDomain.repl — FAMILY H: input domain.
;;
;; "What happens at the edges of what an argument is allowed to be?"
;;
;; Deliberately NOT a fuzzing campaign. A full gate run is ~7 minutes, so random search is an
;; expensive way to find what reading the guards tells you for free. These attacks are targeted at
;; specific gaps identified by reading `SWPU|X>SWAP`, which validates its inputs one at a time and
;; therefore cannot see any property OF THE SET.
;;
;; Run: cd REPL && pact "RedTeam/[RT-H]_InputDomain.repl"
;;
(load "../deploy-stage00.repl")
(load "../deploy-stage01.repl")
(load "../Stage_01/[6.2]_DPTF.repl")
(load "../Stage_01/[6.3]_SWP.repl")

;;|| NEXT >
;;
(begin-tx "RT-H-001 - swap a token for itself, and swap a token twice")
(env-gasmodel "table")(env-gaslimit 100000000)(env-gas 0)
(namespace "ouronet-ns")
;;==== RT-H-001 · 01 · set properties the per-item validator cannot see ====
(print "--- [RT-H-001 · 01 · set properties the per-item validator cannot see] ---")
;;<<RT-H-001>> FAMILY: H | STATUS: FIXED
;;HYPOTHESIS: `SWPU|X>SWAP` -- the capability guarding the NO-SLIPPAGE swap path, which is the one a
;;            caller reaches by passing the `-1.0` sentinel -- validates the output is on the pool,
;;            that swapping is enabled, that input-ids and input-amounts are the same LENGTH, and
;;            then loops over the inputs checking each is on the pool with a valid amount. Every one
;;            of those is a per-ITEM property. Nothing checked a property of the SET. So two things
;;            should be constructible: `output-id` also appearing in `input-ids` (swap a token for
;;            itself), and the same id appearing twice (pay one token, be credited for two).
;;METHOD:     drive both through the live Talos client on a real pool, and record WHICH guard
;;            refuses. The identity of the refusing guard is the entire result here.
;;RESULT:     both were constructible, and both were refused -- but by guards that were NOT about
;;            the set. Fixed 2026-09-14 on the owner's ruling; this block now pins the real rules.
;;
;;WHAT USED TO REFUSE THE SELF-SWAP, and it was not a validator. `output-id` in `input-ids` passed
;;every check in `SWPU|X>SWAP`: it is on the pool, its amount is positive, the lengths match. The
;;swap proceeded INTO the curve math, which computed an output of exactly ZERO -- adding and
;;removing the same token on a constant-function curve nets to nothing -- and the transfer of that
;;zero was then refused by `DPTF::UEV_Amount`'s `(> amount 0.0)`. The transaction was rejected by a
;;ZERO-AMOUNT TRANSFER RULE, three modules away from the mistake, and only because the arithmetic
;;happened to land on exactly 0.0.
;;
;;    That was the finding. The system was not protected from self-swaps by a rule forbidding them;
;;    it was protected by the curve returning zero. Any future change that made a self-swap return
;;    a non-zero positive amount -- a different curve, an amplifier, a fee rebate, a rounding
;;    direction -- would have turned this into value creation with no guard in the way.
;;
;;WHAT USED TO REFUSE THE DUPLICATE was also not a uniqueness rule: "Only a single Input can be used
;;in Stable Swap" is a POOL-TYPE rule. It happened to make duplicates unconstructible on a STABLE
;;pool because stable pools take one input at all. It said nothing about weighted pools, which
;;accept several inputs by design -- so the duplicate case was pinned only for the stable shape and
;;the weighted shape was an open follow-up.
;;
;;THE FIX. `SWPU|X>SWAP` now states both rules directly, as properties of the SET:
;;
;;    (enforce (not (contains output-id input-ids)) "Output Token ... cannot also be an Input ...")
;;    (ref-U|LST::UEV_IzUnique input-ids)
;;
;;Both now refuse IN THE CAPABILITY, before any debit and before the curve is evaluated. That is
;;the difference this block measures: not merely that the attacks fail, but that they fail EARLY
;;and for a reason that names them. The old defences remain in place behind these; they are simply
;;no longer the thing standing between a malformed set and the pool.
(env-sigs [{ "key": "PK_AncientHodler", "caps": [] }])
(let
    (
        (ref-TS01-C3:module{TalosStageOne_ClientThreeV4} TS01-C3)
        (ref-DPTF:module{DemiourgosPactTrueFungibleV2} DPTF)
        (ref-SWP:module{SwapperV4} SWP)
        (ref-SWPU:module{SwapperUsageV3} SWPU)
        (pool:string "S|DLK-98c486052a51|OURO-98c486052a51|DWK-98c486052a51")
        ;;the WEIGHTED twin of the same three tokens, issued by [6.3] at SWP|TX 04. Same tokens,
        ;;different pool type -- so the only thing that differs between attack 2 and attack 3 is
        ;;the rule that gets to answer.
        (wpool:string "W|DLK-98c486052a51|OURO-98c486052a51|DWK-98c486052a51")
        (tok:string "OURO-98c486052a51")
        (other:string "DLK-98c486052a51")
        (me:string KST.ANHD)
    )
    (let
        (
            (mine-before:decimal (ref-DPTF::UR_AccountSupply tok me))
            (pool-before:[decimal] (ref-SWP::UR_PoolTokenSupplies pool))
        )
        (map print
            [
                (format "<<RT-H-001>> holding {} {} against pool {}" [mine-before tok pool-before])
                ;;ATTACK 1 -- swap a token for ITSELF. Now refused by the SET rule that names it,
                ;;in SWPU|X>SWAP, rather than by a zero-amount transfer rule three modules away.
                (expect-failure "<<RT-H-001>> a self-swap is refused by the rule that forbids it"
                    "cannot also be an Input Token"
                    (ref-TS01-C3::SWP|C_MultiSwapNoSlippage me me pool [tok] [100.0] tok))
                ;;ATTACK 2 -- the same input id twice, on the STABLE pool. Still refused by the
                ;;POOL-TYPE rule, and the reason is worth more than the fix was: the new uniqueness
                ;;check never gets to speak here, because the pool-type rule fires UPSTREAM OF THE
                ;;CAPABILITY ENTIRELY. TS01-C3::SWP|C_MultiSwapNoSlippage binds
                ;;
                ;;    (slippage-bounds ... (ref-SWPU::UDC_SpawnSlippageBounds swpair ...))
                ;;
                ;;in a `let` BEFORE it calls SWPU::C_Swap, and a Pact `let` is EAGER -- so the
                ;;curve runs, and the curve's own (= l1 1) stable-pool enforce aborts, before
                ;;SWPU|X>SWAP is ever composed. The validator is downstream of the math it guards.
                ;;The next assertion proves that claim rather than asserting it.
                (expect-failure "<<RT-H-001>> on a STABLE pool the pool-type rule still answers first"
                    "Only a single Input can be used in Stable Swap"
                    (ref-TS01-C3::SWP|C_MultiSwapNoSlippage me me pool [other other] [50.0 50.0] tok))
                ;;WHERE that refusal comes from, demonstrated: the same message falls out of the
                ;;slippage-bounds constructor on its own, with no client call and no capability in
                ;;scope. That is the eager `let` in Talos, not a guard in SWPU.
                (expect-failure "<<RT-H-001>> ...and it comes from Talos's EAGER let, not from the swap capability"
                    "Only a single Input can be used in Stable Swap"
                    (ref-SWPU::UDC_SpawnSlippageBounds pool [other other] [50.0 50.0] tok -1.0))
                ;;ATTACK 3 -- the same input twice on a WEIGHTED pool, which the original pass
                ;;recorded as an OPEN FOLLOW-UP because this block could not reach one. A weighted
                ;;pool accepts several inputs by design, so the pool-type rule has nothing to say
                ;;and the curve does not object. This is the case that had NO guard at all before
                ;;2026-09-14, and it is now refused by uniqueness -- by name.
                (expect-failure "<<RT-H-001>> on a WEIGHTED pool, where nothing objected before, uniqueness answers"
                    "Unique Items Required"
                    (ref-TS01-C3::SWP|C_MultiSwapNoSlippage me me wpool [other other] [50.0 50.0] tok))
                ;;AND NOW "NOTHING MOVED" CAN BE ASSERTED, which it could not be before, and the
                ;;change is the point of the repair rather than a bonus.
                ;;
                ;;RULE 9 says `expect-failure` catches the abort MID-TRANSACTION and does NOT roll
                ;;back writes that already happened. When this block pinned the OLD behaviour, two
                ;;such assertions were written and both FAILED, showing the caller debited by
                ;;exactly the 100.0 self-swap input and the pool's OURO reserve up 0.53:
                ;;
                ;;    caller  389170.206086... -> 389070.206086...
                ;;    pool    [.., 901.591515.., ..] -> [.., 902.121515.., ..]
                ;;
                ;;They were removed as unassertable. They are assertable NOW, and they pass, for a
                ;;reason that is itself the result: the new guards live in the CAPABILITY, so the
                ;;refusal happens before the first write instead of after the debit and the curve.
                ;;RULE 9 has not changed -- there is simply nothing left for it to expose.
                (expect "<<RT-H-001>> the refusal is now EARLY: the caller is not debited at all"
                    mine-before (ref-DPTF::UR_AccountSupply tok me))
                (expect "<<RT-H-001>> ...and the pool reserves are untouched by both attempts"
                    pool-before (ref-SWP::UR_PoolTokenSupplies pool))
                ;;NON-VACUITY: a well-formed swap on this same pool DOES work, so the refusals above
                ;;are about the malformed input sets and not about the pool being unusable.
                (expect "<<RT-H-001>> ...while a well-formed swap on the same pool succeeds"
                    true (> (length (ref-TS01-C3::SWP|C_SingleSwapNoSlippage me me pool other 10.0 tok)) 0))
            ]
        )
    )
    ""
)
(env-sigs [])
(rollback-tx)
